## Abstract

**Background:** Metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer (mCRPC) is 
a highly aggressive disease with poor prognosis and heterogeneous treatment 
response. Short-term mortality risk prediction remains an urgent but 
unsolved challenge, with no reliable tools for identifying patients 
transitioning into a high-risk state warranting proactive care planning.

**Methods:** We developed and externally validated a visit-level 180-day 
mortality risk model using longitudinal data from two Phase III clinical 
trial cohorts (n=526 and n=640). Only visits with observable 180-day 
outcomes were labeled; right-censored cases were excluded. Five candidate 
architectures were compared: Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), Gated Recurrent 
Unit (GRU), Cox Proportional Hazards, Random Survival Forest (RSF), and 
Logistic Regression. For each dataset, the smallest risk threshold achieving 
an 85% sensitivity floor was selected.

**Results:** The GRU and RSF models achieved the highest discrimination in 
the development cohort (C-index: 0.87 for both). In external validation, 
the GRU demonstrated superior calibration (slope: 1.05; intercept: 0.04) 
and achieved a PR-AUC of 0.91 and AUC-ROC of 0.92. Clinical impact analysis 
showed a median time-in-warning of 151.0 days for true positives compared 
to 64.0 days for false positives, with 18.3 alerts per 100 patient-visits. 
Permutation importance ranked BMI and systolic blood pressure as the 
strongest predictors, consistent with late-stage frailty and hemodynamic 
instability.

**Conclusions:** Longitudinal routine clinical markers can estimate 
short-horizon mortality risk in mCRPC with clinically meaningful lead time. 
The GRU framework supports proactive care planning over a multi-month window 
and offers a scalable approach to end-of-life risk stratification in 
late-stage cancer.

**Keywords:** metastatic castration-resistant prostate cancer, mortality 
